From: =?UTF-8?q?Bedirhan=20Gen=C3=A7aslan?=
 <b2210356065@cs.hacettepe.edu.tr>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 14:44:15 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 53/71] Give the Chat wire the apply_patch tool instead of
 dropping it

`apply_patch` is a `ToolSpec::Freeform`, which serializes as `"type": "custom"`
and carries a Lark grammar. `chat_tools_from_responses_tools` kept only
`"type": "function"`, so on this wire the model was told five times to use a
tool it never received, in a format the template never showed -- the grammar is
the only place that format is written down. It improvised the invocation
through the shell, and over four recorded runs got 6 of 37 attempts through.

Z.ai accepts `web_search`, `retrieval` and `function`, and no grammar of any
kind, so the tool now goes out as a function taking the body as a single
`input` string. `sse::chat` unwraps the reply back into a `CustomToolCall`, the
shape every handler above the adapter already expects, and history replays it
in the same shape so the model keeps learning one convention. Nothing outside
`codex-api` changes; the handler, verification and sandbox paths are untouched.

The prompt keeps upstream's `## apply_patch` section, now with upstream's own
opening sentence restored, because the grammar still cannot be sent and the
format has to be written somewhere.

Measured on the `patchmini` task: 3 of 3 patches applied, 7 requests, $0.0011,
against 1 of 18 and $0.0072 through the shell.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
